VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-032
Reason for Visit: Acute severe anxiety and panic attack triggered by the sudden, unexpected death of a similarly aged colleague from a massive myocardial infarction today.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an asymptomatic 48-year-old male experiencing acute, severe somatic symptoms of panic (palpitations, chest tightness, extreme fear of imminent death) entirely provoked by witnessing a colleague's fatal heart attack hours prior. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to physically rule out an acute coronary event in this highly distressed patient, provide immediate clinical reassurance, and channel the acute health anxiety into a structured, comprehensive preventative health assessment to definitively resolve the psychiatric crisis. Delaying assessment would have resulted in an unnecessary ED presentation for a presumed, panic-driven heart attack overnight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "My coworker just dropped dead at his desk from a heart attack today. We are the exact same age, and now my chest is tight and I'm terrified it's happening to me."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents as an essentially asymptomatic adult who experienced a severe acute stress reaction. Following the traumatic workplace event, the patient developed severe health anxiety regarding their own cardiovascular status. The consultation rapidly evolved into a comprehensive structured age-specific health assessment to manage the panic. A systematic evaluation of lifestyle risk factors revealed moderate alcohol consumption, poor nutrition, and minimal physical activity. A measurement of baseline biometrics including blood pressure, weight, height, and body mass index was conducted. A review of current participation in national cancer screening programs and an assessment of family history regarding premature cardiovascular disease were completed. The patient confirms a lack of acute medical complaints driving the consultation prior to the panic attack.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but currently exhibiting high psychological distress.
Social/Safety Concerns: Acute situational panic; spouse is present and highly supportive.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 150/95 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 95 Kg
General: Pacing the room, diaphoretic, hyperventilating slightly, visibly terrified.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm, normal S1/S2, no murmurs, no bruits. Respiratory: Clear air entry bilaterally, no wheeze. Abdominal: Soft, non-tender, no hepatomegaly. The assessment mandated a calculation of the absolute 5-year cardiovascular disease risk score (calculated at moderate risk based on age, BP, and BMI). The clinical presentation involves a lifestyle risk factor evaluation and a discussion of specific preventative interventions. Expected investigation results include normal or actionable baseline screening pathology including a fasting lipid profile and fasting plasma glucose to definitively stratify the cardiovascular risk.
Environmental Observations: Safe, quiet environment; patient's spouse is actively trying to calm them.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Preventive Health Check (Secondary to Acute Health Anxiety / Panic) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tachycardia and mild hypertension directly linked to the psychological trauma of today's event, with complete resolution of the chest tightness upon clinical examination and reassurance.

Generalized Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The intense anxiety is entirely situational and provoked by a specific, catastrophic external trigger today.

Unstable Angina - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of exertional chest pain prior to today; the current symptoms are classic somatic manifestations of acute panic.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced a severe acute stress reaction mimicking a cardiac event, triggered by the sudden death of a peer. Telephone triage accurately identified the need for immediate medical evaluation to rule out true cardiac pathology. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to perform a physical examination, provide undeniable clinical reassurance that the patient was not actively infracting, and immediately pivot the acute fear into a structured, proactive preventative health check. By formally assessing his cardiovascular risk and ordering comprehensive screening pathology, the patient was given tangible control over his health, entirely neutralizing the panic attack and preventing an unwarranted emergency department presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Conducted a thorough physical examination providing immediate reassurance of cardiovascular stability. Performed a structured 45-49 year old health assessment. Generated pathology requests for fasting lipids, fasting glucose, full blood count, and renal/liver function.
Positive Behavioral Change: To harness tonight's fear into a protective habit, commit to a mandatory 20-minute brisk walk every single morning immediately after waking up, before checking your phone or email. Place your walking shoes physically on top of your smartphone at night. This physical barrier forces you to engage with the exercise cue before the stressors of the day begin, actively reducing your long-term cardiovascular risk through consistent, daily conditioning.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 14 days to discuss the fasting pathology results and formalize a long-term lifestyle modification plan.

